eristic makes them particularly useful for forecasting future economic conditions based on historical data. In Sri Lanka, AR models have been employed to analyze trends in economic indicators, such as GDP growth and inflation rates. The reliance on past values allows these models to capture underlying patterns and cycles in the data, which is critical for effective economic planning and policy formulation. [Data: Entities (94, 2112); Relationships (57, 2707)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"The role of Distributed-Lag Models\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Distributed-Lag Models are essential for understanding how the effects of changes in independent variables unfold over time. These models allow researchers to analyze the delayed impact of economic policies or shocks, which is particularly relevant in the context of fiscal and monetary policy in Sri Lanka. By incorporating past values of both dependent and independent variables, Distributed-Lag Models provide a nuanced understanding of economic dynamics, enabling policymakers to make informed decisions based on the temporal effects of their actions. [Data: Entities (95, 2112); Relationships (59, 2712)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Granger Causality Test as a predictive tool\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Granger Causality Test is a statistical method used within Dynamic Econometric Models to determine whether one time series can predict another. This test is particularly valuable in econometrics as it helps identify leading indicators and causal relationships between economic variables. In the context of Sri Lanka, the Granger Causality Test has been applied to assess the predictive relationships between consumption and GDP, providing insights that are crucial for economic forecasting and policy analysis. Understanding these relationships can inform strategic decisions in economic planning. [Data: Entities (96, 2686); Relationships (2711)]\"\n        },\n    